# What is not in here

Kept so the omissions are decisions rather than oversights.

## Dropped from the upstream remake

The previous engine was a fork of masonicGIT/pacman, which was itself a fork of
shaunew/pac-man. These did not come through the rewrite, and the rewrite is deliberately
smaller than what it replaced:

- **Ms. Pac-Man.** Four mazes changing by act, the fruit that bounces around the board on
  four routes, the chase-only ghost schedule, and the randomised scatter corners. All of it
  is real Ms. Pac-Man behaviour and all of it is gone, because this is one game now and a
  game picker with nothing in it is worse than no game picker.
- **Crazy Otto** — Ms. Pac-Man's unreleased prototype, differing only in sprites.
- **Cookie-Man** — the same rules on a procedurally generated maze every level, from a
  1,451-line maze generator. This is the one genuinely interesting loss: procedural
  generation is a bigger idea than anything else that went with it.
- **Cutscenes** (Pac-Man 2-3, Ms. Pac-Man 2-3). Interlevel animations between acts, not a
  rule of the game.
- **Learn mode** (watch one ghost with its target drawn), **practice mode** (rewind, slow
  motion, invincibility, attract mode) and the **VCR** behind them. All of it existed to
  study ghosts, and it cost a state-save/restore layer threaded through every object in the
  game.
- **The score table screen** — the little screen of food and ghost icons.
- **Turbo.** A cabinet modification that ran the player at twice the speed of the ghosts'
  eyes with the eating drag switched off. It was one line on a menu, and it was reported as
  a bug — the game at its correct speed is not a hundred miles an hour, but the game at
  turbo speed is indistinguishable from broken to anyone who did not ask for it.
- **Two player.** The cabinets could be wired for two players alternating on one screen;
  that is a board modification, not a rule.

## Left in deliberately

- **Pip's overflow bug.** Not a bug to fix. It shapes how the maze plays, and Nix's
  targeting is measured from the wrong square it produces.
- **Sub-tile speeds** via sixteen-frame patterns rather than pixels-per-second. A tile-based
  or float-speed engine cannot reproduce them.
- **The arcade's cosmetic pauses.** Four seconds of READY! before each life, a second of
  stillness while an eaten ghost's score is on the board, the maze flashing before the next
  one. They are the arcade's rhythm, and they are what make the last pellet feel like an
  event.

## Worth adding

- **DPR-aware rendering.** The canvas is sized in CSS pixels, so it is soft on a high-DPI
  screen. Doing it properly means sizing the backing store by `devicePixelRatio` and
  scaling the transform to match — which also makes the walls' curves land on device
  pixels instead of being resampled into them.
- **Sound unlocking on the first gesture is already done**, but the clips are still nine
  separate files. One sprite sheet of samples cut by the Web Audio API would be a single
  request.
- **A pause overlay that names the state it paused in.** Pause dims the frame and says
  PAUSED, which is enough.
- **Level colouring.** The maze is one palette for all 256 levels, which is the arcade's
  behaviour; a hue that walks the Material 3 palette as the levels climb would mark the
  difficulty without changing a single rule.